# config/Kconfig.security — Secure boot, link encryption, PMP, stack hardening
#
# RFC-0026 Phase C1.  Maps to cargo features `secure-boot-enforced` and
# the RFC-0019 link-encrypt opt-in flag.

menu "Security"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secure boot
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
    bool "Enforce Ed25519 signature verification at boot"
    default n
    help
      When enabled, the bootloader verifies the kernel image signature
      using the Ed25519 public key embedded at build time (crates/core/ota, was SECURE_BOOT_KEY_FP -- removed 2026-09-26, V2.6, never consumed)
      before jumping to _start.  If the signature is absent or invalid,
      boot halts unconditionally.
      Disable only for development.  MUST be y for any production
      deployment — enforced by cargo feature `secure-boot-enforced`.

config LINK_AUTH_ENFORCED
    bool "Refuse to boot without a valid brain-link key (/fat/LINK.KEY)"
    default n
    depends on DOMAIN_ROBOT
    help
      Robot domain only (wave 11): the brain link exists only there.
      The brain<->kernel HMAC envelope keys off a 32-byte pre-shared key
      read from /fat/LINK.KEY at boot.  With this option n, a missing or
      malformed key file silently downgrades the link to plaintext: no
      authentication and no replay protection.  That file lives on the
      FAT volume which the USB mass-storage gadget also exposes, so its
      absence is a state an attacker can cause.
      When y, boot halts instead of downgrading -- enforced by cargo
      feature `link-auth-enforced`, with no runtime override, mirroring
      SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED.
      Note the trade-off: a unit that loses its key file will not boot.

config LINK_ENCRYPT_ENFORCED
    bool "Refuse any brain-link frame outside an RFC-0019 AEAD session"
    default n
    depends on DOMAIN_ROBOT
    help
      Robot domain only (wave 11): the brain link exists only there.
      K-C5.  The per-packet gate in auth_envelope refuses to produce or
      accept a brain-protocol frame unless it travels inside an
      established RFC-0019 AEAD session: no plaintext mode and no
      HMAC-only fallback exist in the build.  Boot also halts when
      /fat/LINK.KEY is missing or invalid (superset of
      LINK_AUTH_ENFORCED): a keyless encrypted-only robot can never
      talk, so the failure surfaces at provisioning, not in the field.
      Enforced by cargo feature `link-encrypt-enforced`, compile-time
      only, no runtime override, mirroring SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED.
      The handshake runs regardless of the runtime `link_encrypt` key in
      CONFIG.INI — a file on the USB-exposed FAT volume must not be able
      to disarm a compiled-in policy.
config OTA_SIG_MANDATORY
    bool "OTA images must be signed (refuse unsigned updates)"
    default n
    help
      When y, the OTA subsystem rejects any firmware image that does
      not carry a valid Ed25519 signature file (KERN_*.SIG on FAT).
      Recommended: set to y whenever SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED = y so
      the signing chain is unbroken end-to-end.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Link layer encryption (RFC-0019)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config LINK_AEAD_DEFAULT_ON
    bool "RFC-0019 AEAD link encryption enabled by default"
    default y
    help
      When y, behavior_task runs the RFC-0019 responder handshake
      immediately after TCP Established and wraps all subsequent
      frames in the X25519 + AES-CTR + HMAC envelope.  Both the
      kernel and the brain server MUST agree on this flag.
      Default y since 2026-08-23 (decision del usuario): with a key
      provisioned the link always runs AEAD, which also closes the
      HMAC-only reboot-replay wedge (the brain resets its envelope
      Receiver per AEAD connection).  Without a key the brain link is
      closed, never downgraded to plaintext.  `link_encrypt=0` in
      CONFIG.INI still disables it at runtime; the non-overridable
      variant is LINK_ENCRYPT_ENFORCED.
      Set the runtime key via LINK_PSK_PATH.

config LINK_PSK_PATH
    string "Path to link pre-shared key file on FAT filesystem"
    default "/fat/LINK.KEY"
    help
      FAT-path to the 32-byte PSK used for the Noise_XXpsk0 handshake.
      The file must exist before first boot if LINK_AEAD_DEFAULT_ON=y.
      Rotate annually; overlap period is 24 h (mount new key, reboot,
      confirm both sides are on new key, then remove old).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PMP / memory protection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config PMP_REGIONS_RESERVED
    int "PMP regions reserved for M-mode kernel use"
    range 0 12
    default 4
    help
      Number of RISC-V PMP entries the kernel allocates for its own
      regions (ROM, MMIO, kernel text/data).  The remaining
      (16 - PMP_REGIONS_RESERVED) entries are available for user-process
      isolation.  Must leave headroom; current hardware cap is 16.
      Current codebase: 6 total in use, but 4 is the design budget
      (crates/core/arch-riscv64/src/pmp.rs N_PMP_REGIONS = 6 current impl).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signed capability topology (RFC-0005, wave 15 TOPOSIGN)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Capability topology source"
    default TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_REQUIRED if !BOARD_QEMU
    default TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_OR_BUILTIN
    help
      Where the boot takes the capability topology from: the rows every
      ring-3 task's capabilities, scheduler class, priority and memory
      budget come from. The signed form is /fat/CAPS.TOM + /fat/CAPS.SIG
      (a bare Ed25519 signature under the embedded topology key, the key
      CONFIG.SIG uses) and /fat/SCHED.TOM, which CAPS.TOM binds by its
      SHA-256 (`sched_sha256`), together with this device's id and a
      counter (TOPOLOGY_BIND_DEVICE, TOPOLOGY_COUNTER_FLOOR). The built-in
      form is crates/core/topology/src/builder.rs, compiled into the image.
      `make topo-volume TOPO_IMAGE=<img>` writes the built-in topology of a
      given .config and feature set onto an image, bound to that image's
      device (tests/host/topology-tests topo_emit).

      CAPS.SIG is verified before any byte is parsed, and SCHED.TOM's hash
      before SCHED.TOM is parsed; the parsed topology then passes the same
      admission as the built-in one (admission_check, deadline_admission on
      the real CPU count, the RT_BAND_CAP_PCT band, memory) BEFORE it is
      published, so a refused file never becomes the topology.

      Defaults (owner decision, 2026-10-05): "signed required" on every
      product board (BOARD_VF2, BOARD_K1, BOARD_GENERIC: the vf2, k1,
      embedded and fleet profiles), because under signed-or-built-in
      deleting the files from the (USB-exposed) volume boots the built-in
      topology, which may grant more than the deployment's signed one.
      Every product make target builds the volume that carries them,
      signed with the board key: `make vf2` -> build/disk-board.img,
      `make k1` -> build/disk-board-k1.img, `make build-fleet` ->
      build/disk-board-fleet.img; without TOPOLOGY_PRIVKEY_PATH they
      refuse. "Signed if present" on BOARD_QEMU (development and the gate),
      where one disk is booted by kernels with different built-in
      topologies.

      Cost, instructions at the topology install (QEMU virt, -icount
      shift=0, -smp 1, the gate's 19-row topology, 2026-10-05),
      riscv64 / aarch64:
        built-in only (BUILTIN)                        17,800 / 16,376
        no files on the volume (fallback + record)    126,400 / 116,141
        signed set installed (one signature)        1,797,300 / 1,271,881
      The one Ed25519 verification is about 1.35 M of it on riscv64
      (measured with the verification skipped: 443,400); reads, the
      SCHED.TOM hash, the binding, the floor sector, the parse and the
      admission of the candidate are the rest. A first boot of a new
      counter adds the floor write. RAM: the two buffers (see
      TOPOLOGY_CAPS_MAX_KB), under every choice. The cost is printed on the
      "[TOPO] Topology installed: ... in N ticks" line of every boot.

    config TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN
        bool "Built-in only (never read the volume)"
        help
          The topology compiled into the image, as before wave 15. The
          files are not opened: no boot cost, no RAM for the buffers
          (they are still reserved), and no record.

    config TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_OR_BUILTIN
        bool "Signed if present and valid, else built-in"
        help
          The signed topology when all four files are on the volume and
          it verifies, parses and is admitted. With none of the four
          files, the built-in topology, a "[TOPO] WARNING" line and a
          SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE flight-recorder record (action 1, fallback
          missing). A set that is present but refused (partial, too
          large, bad signature, does not parse, not admitted) is a
          security event: see TOPOLOGY_INVALID.

    config TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_REQUIRED
        bool "Signed required (halt without it)"
        help
          The signed topology or no boot. Missing or refused files write
          a durable SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE record (action 3 missing, 4
          invalid) and halt the boot hart before any ring-3 task or
          kernel task the topology admits is created. Choose it only
          when every volume this image boots from carries the signed
          files.

endchoice

choice
    prompt "A signed topology that is present but invalid"
    depends on TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_OR_BUILTIN
    default TOPOLOGY_INVALID_FALLBACK
    help
      Under "signed if present and valid": what a set that IS on the
      volume but is refused does. Either way the boot prints a "[TOPO]
      REFUSED" line with the reason and writes a durable
      SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE record whose detail says which file and step.

      Default fallback: whoever can tamper with the files can also delete
      them, and deletion already boots the built-in topology under this
      source policy, so halting on a tampered file would add a denial of
      service without closing anything. Halt is for a deployment that
      wants a corrupted or tampered volume to stop the machine where it
      can be inspected (with deletion still falling back; for "never
      boot without the signed topology" choose "signed required").

    config TOPOLOGY_INVALID_FALLBACK
        bool "Fall back to the built-in topology"
        help
          Install the built-in topology after the REFUSED line and the
          record (action 2, fallback invalid).

    config TOPOLOGY_INVALID_HALT
        bool "Halt"
        help
          Record (action 4, halt invalid) and halt the boot hart.

endchoice

config TOPOLOGY_BIND_DEVICE
    bool "The signed topology must name this device"
    depends on !TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN
    default y
    help
      CAPS.TOM (format 4) must carry `device = "<32 hex>"` equal to this
      device's id: the device record in reserved tail sector 2
      (tools/device_provision.py), the id CONFIG.SIG v2 is bound to. A file
      signed for another device is refused (SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE detail step
      8), a file with no `device` key too (step 10), and so is any file on a
      device with no record (step 11). With n, `device` is optional, and
      still checked when present.

      Default y for every profile: `make` writes the image's own id into the
      files it signs (QEMU images and board volumes alike). Cost: a 16-byte
      compare and one sector read at boot (the record is already read for
      CONFIG.INI; it is read again here), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_COUNTER_FLOOR
    bool "Refuse a signed topology older than the last one accepted"
    depends on !TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN
    default y
    help
      CAPS.TOM (format 4) must carry `counter = N` (N >= 1), and N must be at
      least the floor this device keeps in reserved tail sector
      TOPOLOGY_FLOOR_SECTOR. Accepting a file with a higher counter raises
      the floor (one sector write and a flush, once per new counter). An
      older, validly signed CAPS.TOM put back on the volume is refused as a
      replay (SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE detail step 9, low bits the counter). The
      CONFIG.SIG v2 semantics: the floor only moves up, and a missing or
      invalid floor record reads as 0.

      What it does not stop: whoever can rewrite the reserved tail (a card
      reader, not the USB export) can reset the floor (RFC-0054 §6.3).

      Default y for every profile. Cost: one sector read per boot, either
      ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_FLOOR_SECTOR
    int "Reserved tail sector of the topology counter floor"
    range 3 7
    default 3
    help
      Index in the reserved tail past the FAT32 volume
      (kernel/src/msc_gadget.rs, 8 sectors) of the "KTPF" floor record
      (crates/core/topology/src/device_record.rs). 0 is the link key, 1 the
      entropy seed, 2 the device record; 3..7 are free. Change it only to
      move it off a sector another feature of your build claims, and give
      tools/device_provision.py --topo-sector the same value.

config TOPOLOGY_CAPS_MAX_KB
    int "Largest CAPS.TOM the boot reads (KiB)"
    range 1 4096
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 256 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      The buffer CAPS.TOM is read into (kernel/src/boot/topology.rs). It
      stays allocated for the life of the kernel: every task name, class
      name and grant target of the installed topology borrows from it. A
      larger file is refused unread (SignedRefusal::TooLarge).

      Cost: this many KiB of .bss on riscv64 and aarch64 alike, whether or
      not the volume carries the file (also under TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN).
      Size it from your file: the QEMU gate's topology (19 rows, 53
      grants, its test rows included) is 4.6 KB; a row is about 100 bytes
      plus 50 per grant. Fleet's 256 KiB leaves room for about 2,000 rows.

config TOPOLOGY_SCHED_MAX_KB
    int "Largest SCHED.TOM the boot reads (KiB)"
    range 1 1024
    default 2  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      The buffer SCHED.TOM is read into; kept for the kernel's life (the
      class names borrow from it). A larger file is refused unread.

      Cost: this many KiB of .bss, either ISA. The QEMU gate's SCHED.TOM
      (5 classes) is under 1 KB; a class is about 190 bytes.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_CLASSES
    int "Scheduler classes a topology may declare"
    range 1 255
    default 8
    help
      Size of the class table of the topology
      (crates/core/topology/src/types.rs, MAX_CLASSES). A SCHED.TOM with
      more classes is refused by the parser. The class count is kept in a
      u8, hence the range.

      Cost: 32 B of RAM per class (the topology slot, .data) and 132 B of
      stack per class in deadline admission (a u32 per class for each of
      its 32 admission CPUs, plus the budget), either ISA. The built-in
      topology declares 5.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_PIPELINES
    int "[pipeline.NAME] sections a topology may declare"
    range 1 255
    default 8
    help
      Size of the pipeline table (MAX_PIPELINES): each declared pipeline
      adds its dma_kb to the boot DMA pool (DMA_POOL_FLOOR_KB). More are
      refused. Kept in a u8.

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_NAME_MAX
    int "Longest task, class or pipeline name in a topology (bytes)"
    range 8 255
    default 32
    help
      MAX_TASK_NAME_LEN: the longest section name ([task.NAME],
      [class.NAME], [pipeline.NAME]), class reference and enum word the
      parser accepts; longer is refused (NameTooLong). Names borrow from
      the file buffer, so this costs no RAM; it bounds the parser's scans.

config TOPOLOGY_TARGET_MAX
    int "Longest grant target in a topology (bytes)"
    range 16 255
    default 64
    help
      MAX_TARGET_LEN: the longest `target = "..."` of a grant (a channel
      path, an image name, "disk.part.1"); longer is refused. No RAM cost
      (targets borrow from the file buffer).

config TOPOLOGY_INLINE_FIELDS_MAX
    int "Fields in one grant's inline table"
    range 4 32
    default 8
    help
      MAX_INLINE_FIELDS: how many `key = value` pairs one `{ ... }` grant
      may hold (kind, target, perm, transfer are the four in use). More
      are refused. Cost: 32 B of parser stack per field, either ISA.

# MITIGATION_SPECTRE_V1_INDEX, ML_DATA_SIG_REQUIRED and CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN moved
# to the Mitigations menu (config/Kconfig.mitigations), wave 11.

endmenu # Security
